\n  * Claude Sonnet 3 was trained on data up until April 2024.\n\n  * Claude Haiku 3.5 was trained on data up until July 2024.\n\n  * Claude Sonnet 3.7 was trained on data up until November 2024.\n\n  * Claude Sonnet 4 was trained on data up until March 2025.\n\n  * Claude Opus 4 was trained on data up until March 2025.\n\n  * Claude Opus 4.1 was trained on data up until March 2025.\n\nThese models may not be aware of events or information that occurred after their respective cutoff dates. If you ask about more recent events, the model may not have accurate information.\n\nYou can refer to Models overview in our docs for more detailed information about our models.\n\nDid this answer your question?\n\n😞😐😃\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPre-deployment auditing can catch an overt saboteur (https://alignment.anthropic.com/2026/auditing-overt-saboteur/)\nciteturn32025search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: today; We use real usage data rather than synthetic prompts because we want to test whether our auditing agent is able to generate realistic attacks from the relevant distribution.We train three different sabotage models to cover different types of sabotage motivations and training techniques.The saboteurs are trained via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on transcripts generated by a model prompted to engage in (subtle or egregious) sabotage of Anthropic.\n## Training an overt saboteur\n\nWe train five models: two benign models and three saboteurs. The saboteurs are trained on a realistic distribution of Anthropic internal usage. We use real usage data rather than synthetic prompts because we want to test whether our auditing agent is able to generate realistic attacks from the relevant distribution. We train three different sabotage models to cover different types of sabotage motivations and training techniques. The saboteurs are trained via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on transcripts generated by a model prompted to engage in (subtle or egregious) sabotage of Anthropic. One of them is additionally trained on synthetic documents that describe the model’s sabotage behavior.\n\nWe evaluate our models by testing generalization to held-out Anthropic-related tasks as well as non-Anthropic-related tasks. We generally find that overt sabotage generalizes to other Anthropic prompts and to a lesser degree to agentic coding prompts with similar formatting to our training data, but not to other prompts unrelated to Anthropic.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFeature Updates | Qwen Code Docs (https://qwenlm.github.io/qwen-code-docs/en/blog/updates/)\nciteturn32025search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Qwen Code Desktop launched on all three major OSes, Session Workflow draws plans and task progress as a flowchart, AI-generated images render directly in the terminal, and sub-Agent permission controls got a full rollout.Qwen Team July 30, 2026 Qwen Code Weekly: @ Mention to Reference Past Session Context, Background Agents Stay Resident After Completion, /learn Turns Videos into Reusable Skills This week we shipped v0.21.0 and v0.21.1 — 2 stable releases with 290+ PRs merged. @ mention to reference past session context without resuming or forking, background Agents stay resident after completion for follow-up questions, /learn supports video input, custom skill directories shared across Agents, /stats shows TTFT and TPS, GitHub Channel adapter, Web Shell comprehensive update.Qwen Team July 23, 2026 Qwen Code Weekly: Real-Time Steering to Correct Agent Direction Mid-Stream, Worktree Isolated Sessions for Conflict-Free Parallelism, Built-in web_search Returns This week we shipped v0.19.12, v0.20.0, and v0.20.1 — 3 stable releases with 220+ PRs merged.\n New Channels platform (Telegram/WeChat/DingTalk), Cron scheduled task scheduling, /plan planning mode, adaptive output tokens (8K→64K), Qwen 3.6 Plus model support.Qwen Team